---
import MainLayout from "../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout>

  <section class="hero">
    <h1 class="sr-only">Closer to Consciousness: The First Strong Theory of Consciousness</h1>
    <img
      src="/c2c/c2c-hero.jpg"
      alt="Closer to Consciousness by Alexander Durig. &quot;Consciousness is the universe's way of getting to know itself.&quot;"
      class="c2c-banner"
    />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The Book</h2>
    <div class="book">
      <figure class="book-cover">
        <img src="/c2c/c2c-cover.jpg" alt="Cover of Closer to Consciousness by Alexander Durig" />
        <figcaption>Cover and Graphic Design by Irina Krasko</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="book-details">
        <p>
          <em>Closer to Consciousness: The First Strong Theory of Consciousness</em>
          (AuthorHouse, 2023) advances a theory of consciousness built on a single proposition:
          the two cerebral hemispheres operate as two interacting brains, each aware of the world
          and of the other's perception of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rather than locating consciousness in one place or one process, the theory treats it as
          arising from the loop between them — a relation the book calls the
          <strong>System of Interactive Reflexivity</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul class="book-facts">
          <li>Paperback, 224 pages · ISBN 9781665579162 · $39.99</li>
          <li>Also available in hardcover and ebook</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="book-links">
          <a class="button" href="https://www.authorhouse.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Available now</a>
          · <a href="/c2c/c2c-poster.pdf">Poster (PDF)</a>
          · <a href="/c2c/blue-ink-review.pdf">Review (PDF)</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Praise</h2>
    <blockquote class="review">
      <p>
        &ldquo;Closer to Consciousness is a serious but accessible work of scholarly substance.
        While it may face challenges from academics entrenched in their own schools of thought,
        it will prove valuable to open-minded students, providing a reset switch in the thinking
        about the most human characteristics of our interior experiences.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <p>
        &ldquo;Has Durig settled the debate? No, but with Closer to Consciousness he's
        re-legitimized the study of consciousness in a way that could ground meaningful
        scientific innovation.&rdquo;
      </p>
      <cite>— Blue Ink Reviews, April 2023</cite>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      <a href="/c2c/blue-ink-review.pdf">Read the full Blue Ink review (PDF)</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>The Core Ideas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Two hemispheres, two brains.</strong> The brain is not one organ doing one thing; the two hemispheres work as interacting systems.</li>
      <li><strong>Interactive reflexivity.</strong> Each side perceives the world and perceives the other perceiving it; consciousness arises from that loop.</li>
      <li><strong>A relational account of mind.</strong> Consciousness is not located at a point; it is a relation sustained between systems.</li>
      <li><strong>The social formation of mind.</strong> Consciousness requires other people. Social interaction shapes meaning, mind, self, language, and emotion.</li>
      <li><strong>A strong claim, held openly.</strong> The book states its claim plainly and engages the field's objections rather than avoiding them.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>From Theory to Architecture</h2>
    <p>
      A theory of consciousness is one thing; a machine built on it is another. Dual Brain
      Robotics is the attempt to give the theory an engineering form.
    </p>
    <p>
      If consciousness arises from two interacting systems working within a three-dimensional
      field of perception, then a machine organized the same way is worth testing. The Dual
      Brain Robotics Architecture gives the two hemispheres a mechanical counterpart — two
      processing modules and an executive module that perceives behavior and adjusts it — and
      sets out to measure whether that organization moves differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      The research program is the test bed. It compares the architecture against traditional
      control across 60 simulations, and reports the results as they come — including where
      the architecture falls short.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>About the Author</h2>
    <p>
      Alexander Durig, Ph.D., is a social psychologist and writer. He is the author of
      <em>Autism and the Crisis of Meaning</em> (SUNY Press, 1996),
      <em>How to Understand Autism</em> (2005), and
      <em>Closer to Consciousness</em> (2023).
    </p>
    <p><a href="/about">More about the author</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>Related</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/architecture">The Architecture</a> ·
      <a href="/research-foundation">Research Foundation</a> ·
      <a href="/patent">Patent Overview</a>
    </p>
  </section>

</MainLayout>

<style>
  .sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  .c2c-banner {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 10px;
  }

  .book {
    display: flex;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .book-cover {
    margin: 0;
    flex: 0 0 260px;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .book-cover img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 6px;
    box-shadow: 0 6px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.18);
  }

  .book-cover figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: #666;
  }

  .book-details {
    flex: 1 1 420px;
  }

  .book-facts {
    color: #444;
  }

  .book-links {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 0.75rem;
  }

  .book-links .button {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .review {
    margin: 0 0 1.25rem 0;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--primary);
    background: #f4f2fa;
    border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;
  }

  .review p {
    margin: 0 0 1rem 0;
  }

  .review cite {
    font-style: normal;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--secondary);
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .book-cover {
      flex-basis: 200px;
    }
  }
</style>
